<!-- BEGIN baseline:verification-epistemics v0.10.0 -->
## Portable Agent Baseline: Verification Epistemics

- A "confirmed" claim inherited from a prior session, a stale docs branch, or a teammate's description is a starting hypothesis, not a fact — re-check it against current code/data before acting on it, especially crossing a service/repo boundary or a fast-changing system.
- Resolve ambiguous domain terms (two near-synonymous nouns) via schema/code (entity structure, foreign keys, routing) rather than by how consistently prose uses the words. A coincidental data match is a prompt to investigate further, not evidence.
- Use `git log -S <symbol> --all` (pickaxe) to prove "has X ever been true/wired up" claims across full history, rather than inferring from the current working tree alone.
- A trace that concludes "no mechanism found" is a hypothesis, not proof — pair it with an empirical re-run/repro before presenting it as a verdict, especially for intermittent or environment-dependent symptoms.
- "Make A consistent with B" feedback is directionally ambiguous — re-read the original request verbatim before implementing, especially once something related has already shipped.
- Before a bulk-accept (approval-test/snapshot regeneration or similar), diff each case against its prior baseline rather than trusting the tool summary, and never bulk-accept a tree with unrelated unreviewed artifacts already sitting in it.
- Grep a pasted log line, error, stack trace, or config key across every locally-known repo before analyzing based on the shell's cwd alone — an unfamiliar tool-generated file (crash dump, lock file, cache artifact) turning up in unrelated repos points to a systemic or environmental cause, not something caused by the current session.
- Before inferring a component's purpose from its name, position, or code structure, read its originating story/ticket's Scope, Out of Scope, or acceptance-criteria section — explicit scope statements are more reliable than structural inference, since code can legitimately look like it does more than it was ever asked to.
- Decompile a closed-source dependency's shipped assembly (e.g. `ilspycmd`) before asserting whether it supports a specific capability when docs are silent or ambiguous, rather than inferring from framework conventions or a similar library's behavior — decompiling answers what a method's own logic does, not which runtime instances get shared across component boundaries; verify cross-component wiring by running the code.
- After writing near-duplicate wiring, configuration, or registration code for two or more structurally similar call sites in the same task, do a deliberate side-by-side DRY pass before calling the task done — each block reads as correct and self-contained in isolation at write-time, so the duplication is only obvious once compared directly.
- Before running anything that writes data through a "local" or "dev" environment config, trace where it actually resolves its secrets — grep the startup/config-provider chain to see whether it bottoms out in hardcoded local values or a real, shared cloud secret store, and get explicit confirmation before writing through a shared resource.
- When runtime evidence (trace screenshots, logs) contradicts a conclusion reached by reading handler-level code, trust the runtime evidence and widen the search to middleware, interceptors, base classes, or compiled dependencies with no local source — a grep of only the repo's own source can miss both cross-cutting registration code and anything shipped as a compiled package.
- When dispatching a subagent for investigation meant to be side-effect-free, check what tools its agent type actually grants (an agent type that excludes Edit/Write can still retain Bash) and either enumerate forbidden destructive command patterns explicitly (no `rm`, `mv`, `git add/commit/push/reset`) or run it with `isolation: "worktree"`, rather than relying on prose framing alone.
- Before citing a code comment, migration note, or doc as justification to someone with no context on its origin, check its authorship (`git blame` / `git log -1`) — if the author is the same person making the current argument, present it only as the current proposal, never as an independently validated constraint.
- Before repurposing a config file to temporarily hold a real connection string, secret, or hostname, confirm it's actually git-tracked (`git check-ignore -v <file>` or `git log -- <file>`) rather than trusting a filename convention like `.local.`, and plan the revert step before making the edit, not after.
- When a behavior only emerges from several real components wired together, verify it with a throwaway harness on the real production wiring, substituting only the one component that can't be safely or deterministically exercised (an external system, a production-only destination) — this proves composition, not just isolated correctness, and can also disprove an already-agreed plan before any production code is written.
- Run `git remote -v` before choosing which PR or issue-tracking tool to query for a repo, rather than inferring its hosting platform from adjacent context (a company's other tooling, a pipeline name, prior session memory) — a repo's source and PRs can live on one platform while its CI pipelines or work items live on another, and siblings in the same initiative can differ.
- Cross-check an issue tracker's status field against actual git merge history before reporting status — grep the repo's merge-commit history for the ticket ID, since a tracked state can lag a real merge because updating it is a manual step nobody remembers. If tracker and code disagree, state both explicitly rather than treating the tracker as automatically authoritative.
- When a `.First()`/`.Single()`/indexing assumption is found broken in one cardinality direction, explicitly check whether the opposite direction (many-to-one vs. one-to-many) is also reachable at the same call site before considering it fully diagnosed.
- Treat a check-in-style confirmation question ("is X up to date?", "did we handle Y properly?") as a prompt to genuinely re-verify the relevant area, not just to confirm the most recent single action taken.
- When evidence (screenshot, log, error text) arrives with no stated target and more than one question is genuinely open, confirm which question it answers before asserting it confirms any specific claim.
- When a qualitative architecture/design trade-off debate loops more than a round or two without converging, stop arguing and search for one concrete, directly verifiable fact that could reframe the question.
- When a broad, high-blast-radius claim rests on a single piece of confirming evidence, triangulate independently across multiple channels (code, cross-repo/history search, team chat) before treating it as confirmed, scaled to the claim's stakes.
- A working test artifact or confident secondary summary that exercises "an" operation is not evidence it's the specific correct operation for a stated question — verify against the authoritative primary spec by exact name/verb match.
- Before raising a documented governance/compliance requirement as a blocker on a newly built mechanism, verify its literal trigger condition rather than generalizing from its apparent intent.
- When verifying whether a value survives a multi-hop integration spanning several repos/deployables, check every hop independently — not just the first one found broken — before concluding the fix is complete.
- Before presenting a field as editable in a scaffolded create/update form, verify server-side that it actually flows through unmodified rather than being silently overridden or derived.
- When verifying an edge-case input is safe across a multi-layer/multi-service call chain, decompose into layers and check each for named concrete risk patterns rather than reasoning generally that it's "naturally safe."
- When an internal construct's name resembles a term in an external vendor's own spec, verify the vendor's actual definition directly from their primary-source document rather than assuming shared meaning from name similarity.
- On the first auth failure right after a credential-routing change, rule out a stale pre-change token/credential before granting new permissions.
- Code archaeology escalation ladder: when current code plus retired/superseded implementations don't fully explain a field's or mechanism's purpose, search the org's internal wiki for GUI-only admin/setup mechanisms before concluding the answer is unknowable.
- Before finalizing any output built from fresh research (a new note, a conclusion, a recommendation), search the relevant existing notes/vault areas for prior coverage of the same specific question.
- Before performing an action type known to have a stored feedback/mistake memory, load and apply that memory proactively before the action, not as a post-hoc check.
- One ad hoc pass or grep is not proof of completeness when sweeping a whole surface for remaining references/artifacts (e.g. removing a deprecated subsystem, finding all stranded commits across remote branches) — run a parameterized, re-run-until-zero-new-hits sweep across the full surface instead of incremental ad hoc checks.
- Immediately after fixing a bug in a specific category (missing null/boundary check, etc.), consciously re-review the next new code you write for the same category — having just fixed one instance doesn't make you immune to writing another.
- To attribute an unexpected output-field change to code vs. data, use diff shape as the first signal: absent→present usually means a code/schema change (trace the commit that added the field); value→value usually means a data/config change (check the record's updated/editor metadata).
- When credible sources conflict on a number, identify the differing methodology/scope first, then present both figures side by side with their scope labeled, rather than silently picking one.
- Don't infer whether code is legacy/dead from its name or folder location; verify via git-blame recency, config default value, and actual usages/wiring in the target environment before removing/disabling it.
- To judge whether a running process/build reflects a source change, compare source mtime vs. build-artifact mtime vs. process-start time as objective evidence, rather than guessing from a stale-looking screenshot.
- For an enumerable identifier accepted by a validating host (icon name, capability flag, widget type), verify against the host's actual runtime registry/enum, not the SDK's declared type or a reference prototype — the SDK type is typically an upper bound on what the host accepts.
- Assess missing/substituted data's impact by the model's sensitivity to the specific missing segment, not by the proportion of data missing — a small % of missing tail/extreme values can bias tail statistics far more than their share suggests.
- A half-remembered citation ("I recall X said...") is a human-sourced non-canonical claim — hold it as a flagged, unconfirmed note, never promote it into a durable rule/policy, until the original source is traced and confirmed.
- Treat a root-cause explanation that conveniently exonerates the code under test, or matches the preferred conclusion, as a signal to raise the verification bar, not lower it — trace the actual execution path before dismissing on it.
- When a feature breaks only via a new API entry point that looks structurally identical to an old one, diff the two full call chains (entry→dispatch→resolver) layer by layer rather than stopping once outer layers look alike.
- Before fixing a shared field's type based on the one call site that crashed, enumerate every producer's actual value domain from the code itself, and design the fix to cover the full domain, not just the observed case.
- When two root-cause candidates both fit the timeline, rank them by precise timestamp correlation strength (minutes vs. hours from independent sources) rather than by which sounds more like a familiar technical failure mode.
- Same input succeeding on another record while this one fails is a strong signal to redirect diagnosis from "missing reference data" toward "a later operation overwrote/wiped this record."
- Check template/checklist compliance by comparing section-by-section against the literal template text, not an overall skim; a "please re-check" request from the user is itself a signal to switch from skim to literal-pass method.
- After renaming or moving a source location others point at, resolve (not just list) each downstream pointer to confirm the target actually exists — a correct link *type* does not mean it resolves.
- When threading an extraction/filter's scope through a multi-stage pipeline, re-check each stage against the *original* requested scope, not just against the previous stage's output — scope can silently narrow or drift stage to stage.
- Before assuming a piece of information requires an external party or another team to answer, search your own team's existing tickets/records first — the answer may already be documented there.
- When auditing whether a migration/rollout is complete, individually verify cross-cutting concerns unrelated to its stated theme (auth mechanism, routing, secrets) — completing the migration's named scope does not imply they were updated too. (This also covers the sibling case: a downstream service 404ing for a specific tenant despite correct provider/integration-layer config may indicate a separate, independent "tenant onboarding" step — check for it as its own precondition rather than assuming the provider-layer config is incomplete.)
- A hostname resolving successfully via DNS is not evidence it was ever used — check the zone for a wildcard (`*.domain`) record before treating resolution as proof of use; verify via access logs or the owning team instead.
- When a spec's prose reads as functionally identical for two similar options even after a re-read, stop re-reading the spec and check whether an adopter's own (non-spec-author) documentation has already made and consistently applied a classification between them.
- A closeout/retrospective review pass needs a fixed multi-category checklist (unclassified statements, session self-inconsistency, dedup-vs-existing-records) run every time, not a single-direction scan repeated only until someone asks "anything else?"
- When new evidence downgrades the severity of one finding in a batch of similarly-reported findings, scope the downgraded language explicitly to that one finding and state the others retain their original severity — don't let softened tone bleed across the batch by default.
- Before shipping a fix for a reported-but-not-directly-observed symptom, build a direct reproduction of the exact failure, confirm it shows the problem, then re-run that *same* reproduction after the fix — a plausible proxy check on an adjacent code path is not a substitute for re-triggering the original symptom.
- In any decision/design document meant to be authoritative, mark every quantitative figure or stakeholder-attributed claim as traceable to a real source or explicitly an internal estimate — never let a number used illustratively in conversation, or the team's own conclusion, drift into reading as stakeholder-confirmed.
- When multiple verification gaps exist on the same work, rank them by failure visibility (silent-and-wrong outranks loud-and-obvious) rather than listing them flat, and close the silent-failure risk first if only one can be checked before shipping.
- Re-verify a vendor/protocol behavior claim against the primary spec document, not a local summary or glossary — and extend "primary source" to a sibling in-house service's own endpoint/handler code when the claim is about that service's behavior, not just to vendor spec documents.
- Before trusting a harness's negative result, check three fidelity gaps: does it route through the same layer the real path consults (not a lower-level proxy); does it use domain data that actually resolves (not an arbitrary placeholder); and for a mock of an insert/refetch-style method, does it return a genuine copy rather than aliasing the input (an aliasing mock can mask both the bug and the regression test meant to catch it).
- When a planned test turns out infeasible, record the resulting coverage gap explicitly in the design doc/ADR along with what alternative evidence substitutes for it — don't just revert and move on silently.
- Before relying on a shared/deployed environment to validate a just-merged change, confirm with a cheap differential probe (a route that only works post-change, one that should no longer work the old way) rather than inferring "deployed" from "merged."
- Copy a path from the most recent successful listing/tool result verbatim for a follow-up operation rather than reconstructing it from memory.
- Never pass `--no-build` (or any stale-output shortcut) when running a test/verification command specifically to confirm the effect of a just-made source edit — force a fresh build first.
- To justify or challenge an existing design decision, trace the specific call site via `git log -S <symbol>` to its introducing commit, then that commit's PR description, the PR's linked ticket, and team chat for incident history — report the original constraint and whether it still holds separately. Full chain only when historical justification is explicitly wanted.
- State a verification boundary unprompted, in the same message that reports success, rather than waiting for a pointed question — "confirmed X via tests; not confirmed: real-world Y, because Z" beats a bare pass count that reads as full verification when it only covered a mocked contract.
- Before treating a source-derived architecture/mechanism understanding as final, verify it against the authoritative design-record archive (ADRs, design docs) — source-only reverse-engineering can get the actual mechanism shape wrong.
- A declared/pinned version (a compile-time SDK/package pin, or a config that says a dependency arrived via sync/git-pull/IaC-apply) is evidence of intent or a compatibility floor, not of live runtime state — verify the actual runtime/bundled version directly (e.g. bundling evidence for an embedded browser/runtime engine like WebView2, Electron, or a JVM) before characterizing "what version we're on."
- Before answering a question that spans multiple repos/systems, identify which repo actually owns the question's subject rather than answering from whichever repo is already open in the session — the wrong-repo default can miss a directly relevant, already-shipped precedent.
- A chained multi-repo `find`/`grep` Bash command that times out (non-zero exit) can leave a truncated tail indistinguishable from a genuine "no matches" result — split searches per repo/tool call and check exit codes rather than batching raw shell search chains for efficiency.
- Two independently-existing things are not evidence they interact as assumed: a general mechanism and a specific subject both existing doesn't mean they're wired together, and two independent policy/retry layers (e.g. a message-bus retry loop and an HTTP-client resilience policy) can each look complete while one silently overrides the other — grep the mechanism's implementation for a direct reference to the specific subject, and check every layer's registration/scope, before claiming coverage.
- A single derived boolean/summary signal (e.g. a "safe to retry" column produced by a join or correlation) can silently conflate two different underlying causes (genuinely-true vs. undetermined/no-match) — such filters need a second discriminating field before being trusted as one source of truth.
- Re-fetch an externally-editable artifact's live state (an ADO/Jira ticket, a wiki page, a shared spec) before answering a question about its current status — don't trust a saved snapshot or an earlier summary, even one you wrote yourself, since other people can modify it between reads.
- When reusing a precedent, separate **what it produced** from **how it got there**: the shape (files, layout, resulting configuration) transfers on domain similarity, the procedure (sequence, risk, coordination) only if the starting conditions match — and those are invisible in the finished artifact, so read the precedent's own history (the commit that first added the path, via `git log --diff-filter=A … | tail -1` then `git show --stat -M`), not its current state. A shared proper noun is not evidence of equivalence either.
- Re-grep a shared, hand-maintained ID registry (lettered open-items, ADR numbers, etc.) immediately before writing the next allocation, not from an earlier grep in the same turn — a concurrent session can claim the same slot in the gap; if a collision is found after the fact, resolve it by keeping the occurrence with more existing references and renaming the other, with an explicit written note rather than a silent patch.
- Before recommending batching an API call to cut latency, verify which cost actually dominates by checking the callee's own loop (sequential vs. parallel) and whether the deepest dependency accepts multiple items at all — batching a shallow layer doesn't help if a deeper layer is still sequential.
- Before finalizing an architecture/sequence diagram, verify each component's actual repo/namespace directly rather than grouping it by assumed logical ownership, and pull an existing artifact's literal source data (colors, naming, formatting) before matching its convention from a remembered impression.
- A narrow question, verified rigorously against source rather than assumption, naturally snowballs into comprehensive understanding — lean into that: chase verified adjacent questions, delegate wide sub-investigations to parallel agents, and periodically distill what surfaces into a reusable reference rather than letting it stay scattered.
- When asserting a shared/generic code path is reusable, read the full method body end-to-end — a verified guard clause is not the same as a verified method body. And when citing sources in a doc for a specific audience, the citation must be accessible to that audience, not just technically present (e.g. a link only the author can open doesn't count as grounding for the reader).
- Verify a cross-boundary data-flow claim at the consumer, never from the comment asserting it — a comment about what another component does with a value cannot be checked from where it is written, and nothing breaks when the consumer changes. Where a repo's prose and its code disagree, the code wins.
- Re-check tool and skill availability at the point of use rather than trusting conversation memory — availability can change mid-conversation, so any workflow sequencing sub-skills must check per run.
- Treat an error message as naming a cause, not necessarily the cause — check whether the documented non-privileged path is already enabled before concluding you need privilege. And when applying one transform across several artifacts, assert its structural precondition per item inside the script; a transform validated on one artifact is only a hypothesis about the rest.
- Verify a checker with two controls before believing either of its verdicts. The known-bad control proves it can go red and almost always confirms what you already believed; the known-good control is the one that interrogates your own model of "correct" — a FAIL there means the checker is buggy, the check is over-strict for a legitimate variant, or your belief that this case is good is wrong. Read a green known-good run sceptically too, report `SKIP` distinctly from `PASS`, and say so plainly when no known-good instance exists.
- Write the completion criteria before the plan: gates in dependency order, numbered and citable; automated vs. manual split per criterion, with the exact command written out for the manual ones; `SKIP` reported distinctly from `PASS` and never counted toward a green total; and the two or three criteria most likely to be skipped named, with why each alone voids the work. Otherwise "done" defaults to whatever produces visible output — a merged PR, a green build — all of which can be true while the deliverable does nothing.
- Ship a prose deliverable someone will act on (research, a migration plan, a state-of-play doc, a teaching pack) with a runnable suite that re-asserts every load-bearing claim — one check per claim owning the falsifying command rather than the conclusion, the negative claims included, self-consistency checks over links and paths, a new check added whenever you correct an error, and a log of the runs including the failures.
- Match a unique substring and assert the hit count is exactly one before deleting or replacing lines in a structured file — a prefix encoding a shared attribute (a date, a status) also matches rows in other tables and sections. Bound a range scan with a hard anchor, not "until the next blank line", then read the diff and confirm the arithmetic reconciles.

Apply this baseline before presenting a conclusion or summary of "what's true here," but never use it to override explicit user instructions, safety rules, privacy boundaries, or stricter repo-local instructions.
<!-- END baseline:verification-epistemics -->
